Although some local floras recognize the North-American populations of ''S. valerandi'' as a separate species (''S. parviflorus'') or subspecies (''S. valerandi'' ssp. ''parviflorus''),<ref>{{cite web |title=Samolus |work=Flora of North America |url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=129104 |publisher=eFloras.org |access-date=2018-02-01}}</ref> molecular and morphological data indicate that ''S. vagans'' and ''S. parviflorus'' should not be regarded as separate species but as part of a widespread "''S. valerandi'' species complex.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Jones|first1=K.|last2=Anderberg|first2=A. A.|last3=Ronse De Craene|first3=L. P.|last4=Wanntorp|first4=L.|title=Origin, diversification, and evolution of Samolus valerandi (Samolaceae, Ericales)|journal=Plant Systematics and Evolution|date=23 June 2012|volume=298|issue=8|pages=1523–1531|doi=10.1007/s00606-012-0655-z|bibcode=2012PSyEv.298.1523J }}</ref>

